 🛵 Food Delivery Time Prediction Using Logistic Regression

📌 Project Overview

This project uses Machine Learning and Logistic Regression to predict whether a food delivery will be quicker or slower based on different factors such as distance, weather, traffic conditions, preparation time, vehicle type, and courier experience.

The main objective is to classify food deliveries into two categories using historical delivery data and evaluate the model's performance using standard classification metrics.

🎯 Objectives

- Analyze food delivery data and identify important features.
- Perform data cleaning and preprocessing.
- Convert delivery time into a binary classification problem.
- Train a Logistic Regression classification model.
- Evaluate the model using Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score, and ROC-AUC.
- Understand how different factors influence delivery speed.

📂 Dataset

Dataset Name: Food Delivery Time Prediction

Source: Kaggle

🔗 "Download Food Delivery Time Prediction Dataset" (https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/denkuznetz/food-delivery-time-prediction)

The dataset contains 1,000 records and 9 original columns.

Features Used

Feature| Description
Distance_km| Distance of delivery in kilometers
Weather| Weather conditions
Traffic_Level| Traffic conditions
Time_of_Day| Time when the order is placed
Vehicle_Type| Type of delivery vehicle
Preparation_Time_min| Food preparation time
Courier_Experience_yrs| Courier's experience

Target Variable: "Delivery_Class"

- "0" – Quicker Delivery (below 55.5 minutes)
- "1" – Slower Delivery (55.5 minutes or above)

The median delivery time of 55.5 minutes is used to create the two classes.

🛠️ Technologies Used

- Python
- Google Colab / Jupyter Notebook
- Pandas
- NumPy
- Matplotlib
- Scikit-learn
- Logistic Regression
- Machine Learning

⚙️ Project Workflow

1. Data Loading and Exploration

- Loaded the dataset using Pandas.
- Examined dataset shape and column information.
- Checked missing values and duplicate records.
- Analyzed descriptive statistics.

2. Data Preprocessing

- Removed the "Order_ID" column because it is only an identifier.
- Handled missing numerical values using median imputation.
- Handled missing categorical values using mode imputation.
- Converted categorical features into numerical values using One-Hot Encoding.
- Applied StandardScaler to normalize feature values.

3. Target Variable Creation

Converted the original delivery time into two classes using the median value (55.5 minutes).

- Below 55.5 minutes: Quicker Delivery.
- 55.5 minutes or above: Slower Delivery.

4. Train-Test Split

The dataset was divided using an 80:20 ratio.

- Training Data: 800 records (80%)
- Testing Data: 200 records (20%)

Stratified sampling was used to maintain class distribution.

5. Model Training

A Logistic Regression model was trained using Scikit-learn with "max_iter=2000" and "random_state=42".

6. Model Evaluation

The trained model was evaluated using:

- Accuracy
- Precision
- Recall
- F1-score
- ROC-AUC
- Confusion Matrix
- ROC Curve

📊 Model Performance

The Logistic Regression model achieved the following results on the test dataset:

Evaluation Metric| Score
Accuracy| 92.00%
Precision| 90.38%
Recall| 94.00%
F1-score| 92.16%
ROC-AUC| 0.9692

Understanding the Results

- Accuracy (92%): The model correctly classified 92% of test observations.
- Precision (90.38%): Around 90.38% of predicted slower deliveries were actually slower.
- Recall (94%): The model identified 94% of actual slower deliveries.
- F1-score (92.16%): Shows a good balance between precision and recall.
- ROC-AUC (0.9692): Indicates excellent ability to distinguish between quicker and slower deliveries.

🎚️ Decision Threshold

The default decision threshold of 0.50 was used for classification.

A delivery is classified as slower when its predicted probability of belonging to the slower delivery class is at least 50%.

This threshold provides a reasonable baseline balance between precision and recall. False negatives are particularly important because a delayed delivery predicted as quicker can create incorrect customer expectations.

📈 Visualizations

The project includes:

1. Confusion Matrix – Displays correct and incorrect classifications.
2. ROC Curve – Shows the model's ability to distinguish between the two classes.

